---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Geometric and Systems Intuition for Modern Wireless PHY - Solo Ghost"
  description="A systems-level view of OFDM, MIMO, Doppler, covariance, estimation, and adaptive wireless receivers."
>
  <article class="note">
    <header class="note-hero">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">TECHNICAL NOTE 01</p>
        <h1>Geometric and Systems Intuition for Modern Wireless PHY</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          A systems-level view of OFDM, MIMO, Doppler, covariance, estimation,
          and adaptive receivers.
        </p>
        <div class="note-meta">
          <span>SOLO GHOST</span>
          <span>TECHNICAL NOTE</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">1 · WHY LOOK AT PHY GEOMETRICALLY?</p>
        <p>
          Wireless receivers are often taught as a sequence of processing
          blocks: synchronization, CP removal, FFT, channel estimation,
          equalization, detection, and decoding.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each block has its own mathematics. But a more useful systems-level
          interpretation is to ask:
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          What is each receiver operation doing to the representation of the signal?
        </blockquote>
        <p>This reveals a common structure:</p>
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div><strong>DFT</strong><span>Projection onto frequency basis functions</span></div>
          <div><strong>SVD</strong><span>Decomposition into spatial directions</span></div>
          <div><strong>Covariance</strong><span>Statistical structure and uncertainty</span></div>
          <div><strong>Estimation</strong><span>Inference of hidden channel or state</span></div>
          <div><strong>Equalization</strong><span>Compensation for channel-induced distortion</span></div>
          <div><strong>Tracking</strong><span>Following an evolving state</span></div>
          <div><strong>Beamforming</strong><span>Steering energy in selected spatial directions</span></div>
          <div><strong>Detection</strong><span>Deciding which valid signal point best explains an observation</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">2 · OFDM AS A COORDINATE TRANSFORMATION</p>
        <p>
          An OFDM symbol can be viewed as a linear combination of orthogonal
          complex exponential basis functions:
        </p>
        <pre><code>φₖ[n] = eʲ²πkn/N
x[n] = Σₖ X[k] φₖ[n]</code></pre>
        <p>
          The basis function is the oscillation pattern. The projection onto
          that basis produces the complex coefficient X[k]. The coefficient
          represents the magnitude and phase of that frequency component.
        </p>
        <p>
          Geometrically, the DFT changes the coordinates used to represent the
          same signal. The time-domain waveform and frequency-domain
          coefficients are two representations of the underlying signal.
        </p>
        <h2>Why can OFDM separate subcarriers?</h2>
        <p>
          The OFDM basis functions are orthogonal over the observation
          interval. Ideally, the inner product between two different basis
          functions is zero. Projection onto one basis therefore does not
          collect energy from another.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          Orthogonality is the geometric reason OFDM can separate subcarriers.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">3 · CP, CONVOLUTION AND THE FREQUENCY-DOMAIN CHANNEL</p>
        <p>
          A multipath SISO channel can be represented by an impulse response
          h[0], h[1], …, h[L−1]. Without a cyclic prefix, transmission through
          the channel produces linear convolution and can be represented by a
          Toeplitz convolution matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          With a sufficiently long cyclic prefix, the useful received
		  OFDM block can be represented as circular convolution. The DFT 
		  then diagonalizes that circular convolution operator. The DFT then 
		  diagonalizes that circular convolution operator.
        </p>
        <pre><code>linear convolution
        ↓
cyclic prefix
        ↓
circular convolution
        ↓
DFT
        ↓
diagonal frequency-domain channel</code></pre>
        <p>The familiar per-subcarrier relationship follows:</p>
        <pre><code>Y[k] = H[k] X[k] + N[k]</code></pre>
        <p>
          For MIMO, each transmit-receive antenna pair has its own time-domain
          impulse response. Applying the DFT to each pair produces one MIMO
          channel matrix H[k] for each subcarrier.
        </p>
        <pre><code>time-domain MIMO impulse responses
              ↓
      CP / circular structure
              ↓
       DFT across delay
              ↓
       H[0], H[1], …, H[N−1]
              ↓
   one MIMO channel matrix per subcarrier</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">4 · FREQUENCY, SPACE AND TIME ARE DIFFERENT DIMENSIONS</p>
        <p>OFDM-MIMO processing simultaneously involves several dimensions:</p>
        <div class="dimension-list">
          <div><strong>Frequency</strong><span>Subcarriers</span></div>
          <div><strong>Space</strong><span>Transmit/receive antenna directions</span></div>
          <div><strong>Time</strong><span>Channel evolution</span></div>
          <div><strong>Delay</strong><span>Multipath structure</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The DFT and SVD should therefore not be confused. The DFT transforms
          between time and frequency representations. The SVD decomposes a
          MIMO channel matrix into spatial directions.
        </p>
        <pre><code>DFT
  → frequency representation

SVD
  → spatial channel modes

Covariance
  → statistical structure</code></pre>
        <p>
          For each subcarrier, a MIMO channel can be decomposed as:
        </p>
        <pre><code>H[k] = U[k] Σ[k] Vᴴ[k]</code></pre>
        <p>
          The singular vectors describe transmit- and receive-side spatial
          directions, while the singular values describe the strengths of the
          corresponding spatial modes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">5 · FREQUENCY SELECTIVITY IS NOT THE SAME AS TIME VARIATION</p>
        <div class="compare">
          <div>
            <strong>Frequency-selective</strong>
            <p>The channel response varies with frequency.</p>
            <code>H[k₁] ≠ H[k₂]</code>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Time-varying</strong>
            <p>The channel changes with time.</p>
            <code>H[k,t₁] ≠ H[k,t₂]</code>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Frequency selectivity is primarily associated with multipath delay
          spread. Time variation can arise from Doppler, mobility, changing
          geometry, and changing propagation paths.
        </p>
        <p>
          A channel can therefore be frequency-selective and stationary,
          frequency-flat and time-varying, or both frequency-selective and
          time-varying.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">6 · COVARIANCE: STRUCTURE INSIDE RANDOMNESS</p>
        <p>
          Covariance measures how quantities vary together. For a random vector
          x, its covariance matrix is:
        </p>
        <pre><code>Rₓ = E[(x − μ)(x − μ)ᴴ]</code></pre>
        <p>
          The covariance matrix therefore contains the pairwise statistical
          relationships among the components.
        </p>
        <p>
          The covariance matrix encodes statistical structure; its 
		  eigendecomposition exposes the principal directions of that structure.
          A common next step is eigendecomposition:
        </p>
        <pre><code>R = Q Λ Qᴴ

eigenvectors → principal directions
eigenvalues  → variance / power along those directions</code></pre>
        <blockquote>
          Covariance describes the statistical structure; eigendecomposition
          reveals the dominant directions of that structure.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">7 · COVARIANCE IS NOT AN ICI MATRIX</p>
        <p>
          Under ideal OFDM conditions, the frequency-domain channel is
          approximately diagonal in the subcarrier domain. Under time
          variation during the OFDM symbol, off-diagonal terms can appear:
        </p>
        <pre><code>Y[k] = Σₘ H[k,m] X[m] + N[k]

ideal:
H[k,m] ≈ 0  for k ≠ m

time-varying:
H[k,m] ≠ 0  for k ≠ m</code></pre>
        <div class="compare">
          <div><strong>Covariance matrix</strong><p>Statistical relationship among random quantities.</p></div>
          <div><strong>ICI / channel coupling matrix</strong><p>Effective coupling between subcarrier components.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The two can be statistically related, but they are different
          mathematical objects.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">8 · WHAT DOPPLER DOES GEOMETRICALLY</p>
        <p>
          Doppler causes the channel to vary during the OFDM observation
          interval. A simplified Doppler contribution can be represented by a
          time-dependent phase:
        </p>
        <pre><code>eʲ²πfᴰt</code></pre>
        <pre><code>ideal case

projection onto basis k
        ↓
mostly coefficient X[k]


time-varying channel

projection onto basis k
        ↓
desired component
+
energy from other basis components</code></pre>
        <p>
          That leakage is the geometric origin of ICI.
        </p>
        <p>
          This should be kept separate from MIMO eigenstructure evolution.
          Doppler can degrade Fourier/subcarrier orthogonality, while channel
          evolution can cause spatial singular vectors or eigenmodes to change.
          Both phenomena can occur simultaneously, but they are not the same
          mechanism.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">9 · CORRELATION, COHERENCE AND ESTIMATION VALIDITY</p>
        <blockquote>
          How long or how far does a useful relationship in the signal or
          channel remain sufficiently preserved?
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Temporal correlation provides a mathematical basis for understanding
          temporal coherence. High correlation magnitude indicates strong
          similarity; low magnitude indicates weaker similarity.
        </p>
        <pre><code>channel estimate
      ↓
useful for some interval
      ↓
channel evolves
      ↓
estimate becomes stale</code></pre>
        <p>
          This is the practical meaning of estimation validity. The useful
          interval depends on Doppler, channel statistics, required accuracy,
          estimator behavior, and system design.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">10 · ESTIMATION AS GEOMETRIC INFERENCE</p>
        <p>A receiver often observes a noisy relationship such as:</p>
        <pre><code>y = f(x, θ) + n</code></pre>
        <p>
          where θ represents an unknown channel or state. The receiver seeks
          an explanation of the observations that is consistent with the
          assumed model and uncertainty.
        </p>
        <pre><code>known pilot
    ↓
unknown channel
    ↓
noisy observation
    ↓
channel estimate</code></pre>
        <p>
          Under the appropriate Hilbert-space interpretation, MMSE estimation
          can be viewed as a projection onto the information available to the
          estimator.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">11 · KALMAN FILTERING AS EVOLVING INFERENCE</p>
        <p>A state-space model describes a hidden state that evolves over time and produces observations:</p>
        <pre><code>xₖ = A xₖ₋₁ + wₖ
yₖ = H xₖ + vₖ</code></pre>
        <pre><code>previous state estimate
        ↓
     predict
        ↓
 new observation
        ↓
     correct
        ↓
updated state estimate
        ↓
      repeat</code></pre>
        <p>
          Its covariance represents uncertainty about the state estimate.
          Geometrically, uncertainty can be imagined as an evolving ellipsoid:
          prediction expands or transforms uncertainty, while measurements
          contract it along observed directions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">12 · THE RECEIVER AS A DYNAMICAL SYSTEM</p>
        <pre><code>physical environment
        ↓
time-varying channel
        ↓
observations
        ↓
estimation
        ↓
state / channel representation
        ↓
prediction
        ↓
adaptation
        ↓
new observation
        ↺</code></pre>
        <p>
          Under mobility, the receiver is not merely processing independent
          packets. It is continuously trying to infer and track an evolving
          physical state.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          Modern PHY can increasingly be understood as state estimation and
          adaptive inference over an evolving physical system.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">13 · FROM REACTIVE PHY TO PREDICTIVE PHY</p>
        <pre><code>channel changes
      ↓
estimate degradation
      ↓
update receiver</code></pre>
        <pre><code>observe channel evolution
      ↓
infer dynamics
      ↓
predict degradation
      ↓
adapt before failure</code></pre>
        <p>
          Prediction should not be separated from confidence. A useful adaptive
          architecture can combine prediction with a measure of how reliable
          that prediction is.
        </p>
        <pre><code>high confidence
    ↓
predictive adaptation

low confidence
    ↓
robust / classical fallback</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">14 · TOWARD AI-NATIVE PHY</p>
        <p>
          An AI-native PHY does not necessarily mean replacing DSP with a large
          neural network.
        </p>
        <pre><code>classical DSP
     +
structured estimator
     +
lightweight learned predictor
     +
confidence mechanism
     +
adaptive control</code></pre>
        <p>
          A practical architecture can preserve deterministic components,
          interpretability and existing PHY infrastructure while using learned
          structure where it provides useful prediction or adaptation.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          Can useful learned structure be compressed into a deterministic,
          low-latency inference block?
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">15 · A UNIFIED MENTAL MODEL</p>
        <pre><code>                 PHYSICAL CHANNEL
                       │
             ┌─────────┴─────────┐
             │                   │
          multipath             motion
             │                   │
           delay              Doppler
             │                   │
             └─────────┬─────────┘
                       ↓
                time-varying MIMO
                     channel
                       │
                       ↓
                OFDM / DFT view
                       │
                       ↓
                    H[k,t]
                       │
              ┌────────┴────────┐
              │                 │
          frequency           spatial
          structure           structure
              │                 │
          correlation           SVD
              │                 │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       ↓
                   estimation
                       │
                   covariance
                       │
                       ↓
                    tracking
                       │
                   prediction
                       │
                   confidence
                       │
                       ↓
                  PHY adaptation
                       │
                       ↓
                 new observation
                       ↺</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">16 · CORE PRINCIPLE</p>
        <blockquote class="large-quote">
          Do not treat channel variation as arbitrary randomness when part of
          that variation is physically structured and therefore predictable.
        </blockquote>
        <p>Four questions follow:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>What structure is predictable?</li>
          <li>Over what horizon?</li>
          <li>With what confidence?</li>
          <li>At what computational cost?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These questions bring estimation theory, stochastic processes, linear
          algebra, DSP, control and machine learning into the same receiver
          problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          The long-term objective is not simply faster signal processing. It is
          maintaining a reliable representation of an evolving wireless channel
          and adapting the receiver before that representation becomes stale.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="note-footer">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">SOLO GHOST</p>
        <p>
          This note is a systems-level conceptual synthesis. Detailed
          experimental evidence will be developed in subsequent technical
          notes and engineering artifacts.
        </p>
        <div class="footer-links">
          <a href="/research/">Research →</a>
          <a href="/artifacts/">Engineering artifacts →</a>
          <a href="/ip/">Intellectual property →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

